    <item>
      <title>Declarative Operators: A New Model for Kubernetes Extensibility</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/publications/declarative-operators-whitepaper/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/publications/declarative-operators-whitepaper/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Orkestra Project — March 2026&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;abstract&#34;&gt;Abstract&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Kubernetes operators encode domain knowledge as reconciliation logic. Every&#xA;major operator framework to date requires this logic to be written in a&#xA;programming language, compiled into a binary, and deployed as a separate&#xA;long-running process — one per CRD. The result, at scale, is operator sprawl:&#xA;dozens of binaries each with independent lifecycle, observability, and&#xA;resource consumption.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This paper presents a different model. We argue that the operator pattern&#xA;is fundamentally correct, but its implementation has been conflated with its&#xA;mechanism. The pattern requires one reconciler per CRD with proper isolation.&#xA;It does not require one binary per CRD. When a shared runtime provides the&#xA;isolation, each CRD becomes a complete, independent operator — with its own&#xA;informer, worker pool, workqueue, health endpoint, and metrics — while the&#xA;operational overhead of running them collapses to a single process.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Resource Conditions</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/conditional/resource-conditions/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/conditional/resource-conditions/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Attach a &lt;code&gt;when:&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;anyOf:&lt;/code&gt; block to any resource declaration under &lt;code&gt;onCreate&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onReconcile&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;onDelete&lt;/code&gt;. Orkestra evaluates it before creating, updating, or skipping that resource.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;basic-usage&#34;&gt;Basic usage&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;operatorBox&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;crdFile&lt;/span&gt;: my-operator-crd.yaml&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;onCreate&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;{{ .metadata.name }}-public&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;: LoadBalancer&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;port&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;80&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;targetPort&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;{{ .spec.port }}&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;reconcile&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;          - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;field&lt;/span&gt;: spec.exposePublicly&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;            &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;equals&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;true&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;If the CR has &lt;code&gt;spec.exposePublicly: &amp;quot;false&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt;, the service is skipped. No error. No partial state.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;multiple-conditions-and&#34;&gt;Multiple conditions (AND)&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;All conditions in &lt;code&gt;when:&lt;/code&gt; must pass:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Why Orkestra?</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/blog/01-why-orkestra/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/blog/01-why-orkestra/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Kubernetes has always promised declarative infrastructure. You describe what you want. The platform makes it so.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;That promise holds everywhere — until you need to extend Kubernetes itself.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The moment you need a custom resource, you leave the declarative world. You write Go. You scaffold controllers. You wire informers and schemes. You manage reconcile loops, retries, and backoff. You build and push images. You maintain a project whose entire purpose is to watch another project.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Async Reconciliation</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/conditional/async-reconciliation/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 28 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/conditional/async-reconciliation/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Orkestra&amp;rsquo;s reconciler is synchronous — it receives a CR key, runs, and returns. Real systems are not: a Deployment takes time to become ready, a database takes time to accept connections.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;when:&lt;/code&gt; gate on &lt;code&gt;onReconcile&lt;/code&gt; bridges this gap. If the condition is not met, the block is skipped and the CR is automatically requeued when the child resource&amp;rsquo;s state changes. No sleeping. No polling. No custom state machine.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-pattern&#34;&gt;The pattern&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;operatorBox&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;crdFile&lt;/span&gt;: my-operator-crd.yaml&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#6272a4&#34;&gt;# Phase 1 — runs once on CR creation&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;onCreate&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;deployments&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;{{ .metadata.name }}&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;image&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;{{ .spec.image }}&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;replicas&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;{{ .spec.replicas }}&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;reconcile&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#6272a4&#34;&gt;# Phase 2 — runs only when Phase 1 is complete&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;onReconcile&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;{{ .metadata.name }}-svc&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;port&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#bd93f9&#34;&gt;80&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;targetPort&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#bd93f9&#34;&gt;8080&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;reconcile&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;          - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;field&lt;/span&gt;: children.deployment.status.readyReplicas&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;            &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;equals&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;{{ .spec.replicas }}&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;What happens:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Your CRD Is Enough</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/blog/02-your-crd-is-enough/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/blog/02-your-crd-is-enough/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The engineering argument for why the operator should have always been the CRD.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In 2017, Kubernetes shipped Custom Resource Definitions as the stable mechanism for extending its API. The design was deliberate. A CRD is not a plugin — it is a declaration. You tell Kubernetes: there is a new kind of object in the world, it belongs to this group, it looks like this schema. Kubernetes responds by doing everything it already knows how to do with objects: storing them, serving them, watching them, validating them. All of the platform&amp;rsquo;s machinery applies automatically to your new type.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>The Konductor: How the CLI Became the Orkestrator</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/publications/the-function-that-waited/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 28 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/publications/the-function-that-waited/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Orkestra Project — May 2026&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;a-function-that-waited&#34;&gt;A function that waited&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34; data-lang=&#34;go&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#6272a4&#34;&gt;// KonduktorKind returns the kind string for a Konduktor document.&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#8be9fd;font-style:italic&#34;&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#50fa7b&#34;&gt;KonduktorKind&lt;/span&gt;() &lt;span style=&#34;color:#8be9fd&#34;&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; {&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; kindKonductor&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;This function lived in the konfig package for a long time without a caller.&#xA;The name felt right. The concept felt real. But the implementation it was&#xA;pointing at — the thing that would make a Konductor concrete — was not yet&#xA;visible.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The obvious candidate was leader election. In any distributed system, one&#xA;instance becomes active and the others stand by. Orkestra calls that instance&#xA;the active Konductor. It reconciles. The others hold warm caches and wait.&#xA;That is a Konductor in the operational sense: the one currently conducting.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Status Conditions</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/conditional/status-conditions/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/conditional/status-conditions/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;status.fields&lt;/code&gt; entries accept an optional &lt;code&gt;when:&lt;/code&gt; block. The field is written only when all conditions pass. Multiple entries for the same path are evaluated in order — the last one whose conditions pass writes the final value.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is how Orkestra implements declarative state machines.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;basic-state-machine&#34;&gt;Basic state machine&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;fields&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;: phase&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Pending&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;field&lt;/span&gt;: status.phase&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;          &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;operator&lt;/span&gt;: notExists    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#6272a4&#34;&gt;# only on first reconcile&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;: phase&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Running&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;field&lt;/span&gt;: status.phase&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;          &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;equals&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Pending&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;: phase&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Succeeded&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;field&lt;/span&gt;: status.phase&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;          &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;equals&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Running&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;field&lt;/span&gt;: children.job.status.succeeded&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;          &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;operator&lt;/span&gt;: gt&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;          &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;0&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;: phase&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Failed&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;field&lt;/span&gt;: children.job.status.failed&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;          &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;operator&lt;/span&gt;: gt&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;          &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;0&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Transitions:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Why Operators Are Over-Permissioned — And How We Fixed It</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/blog/03-security-by-default/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/blog/03-security-by-default/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Most Kubernetes operators are massively over-permissioned. Not slightly, not accidentally — structurally. Inspect the RBAC of a typical production operator and you will find something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;- &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;apiGroups&lt;/span&gt;: [&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;*&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;]&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;resources&lt;/span&gt;: [&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;*&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;]&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;verbs&lt;/span&gt;: [&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;*&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;]&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Cluster-wide. Full access. And it is not because the engineers who wrote them were careless. It is because the system makes it easier to be unsafe than correct.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The operator model evolved around one assumption: an operator is a program. You write Go, scaffold controllers, wire informers. Somewhere in that process you add RBAC. Then you add more. Then you hit a permissions error in production at 2am and do the fastest thing possible — widen the scope until it works. And it stays that way. Not because it is right, but because RBAC is painful to maintain, operators evolve, and nobody wants to debug fine-grained permissions under pressure. So teams converge on a pattern: over-permission once, never touch it again.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Declaring Dependencies</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/dependency-model/declaring-dependencies/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/dependency-model/declaring-dependencies/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;In your Katalog, you declare dependencies with &lt;code&gt;dependsOn&lt;/code&gt;. Three formats are supported — use whichever fits your style.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;format-1--simple-list&#34;&gt;Format 1 — Simple list&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The most common format. Each entry is a CRD name. The condition defaults to &lt;code&gt;started&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;crds&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;database&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;crdFile&lt;/span&gt;: database-crd.yaml&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;application&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;crdFile&lt;/span&gt;: application-crd.yaml&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;dependsOn&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;database      # condition&lt;/span&gt;: started (default)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;format-2--key-value-map&#34;&gt;Format 2 — Key-value map&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When you need different conditions per dependency:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;crds&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;application&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;dependsOn&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;database&lt;/span&gt;: started&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;cache&lt;/span&gt;: ready&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;format-3--full-map&#34;&gt;Format 3 — Full map&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The most explicit form. Useful when tooling or schema validation requires structured objects:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lifecycle</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/dependency-model/lifecycle/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/dependency-model/lifecycle/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;how-orkestra-builds-the-dependency-graph&#34;&gt;How Orkestra builds the dependency graph&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When Orkestra starts, it reads all CRD entries and their &lt;code&gt;dependsOn&lt;/code&gt; declarations and builds a directed acyclic graph (DAG) — each node is a CRD, each edge points from a dependency to its dependent.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-text&#34; data-lang=&#34;text&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;database ──┐&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;           │&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;           ▼&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;     application&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;From the graph, Orkestra computes a topological sort — the startup order:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;database&lt;/code&gt; starts first (no dependencies)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;application&lt;/code&gt; starts second (waits for &lt;code&gt;database&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For each CRD, Orkestra creates a ready channel — a signal that fires when the CRD meets its declared condition. Dependents block on their dependencies&amp;rsquo; channels before starting workers.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>CRD Health</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/health-subsystem/crd-health/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 28 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/health-subsystem/crd-health/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Each operatorBox tracks its own health state independently using a &lt;code&gt;CRDHealth&lt;/code&gt; instance. Health is updated on every reconcile cycle.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;what-crd-health-tracks&#34;&gt;What CRD health tracks&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;table&gt;&#xA;  &lt;thead&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th&gt;Field&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;          &lt;th&gt;Description&lt;/th&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/thead&gt;&#xA;  &lt;tbody&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;started&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Whether the reconciler has begun processing events&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;healthy&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Whether the reconciler is currently considered healthy&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;totalReconciles&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Total reconcile attempts&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;failedReconciles&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Number of failed reconciles&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;consecutiveFails&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Consecutive failure counter — drives degradation&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;lastError&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Last error message&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;lastReconcile&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;Timestamp of last reconcile&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;      &lt;tr&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;startTime&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;          &lt;td&gt;When the reconciler first started&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;      &lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/tbody&gt;&#xA;&lt;/table&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;All fields are atomic and safe for concurrent updates from multiple workers.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Autoscaler Condition Engine</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/autoscaler-condition-engine/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 28 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/autoscaler-condition-engine/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;How Orkestra evaluates autoscale conditions.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The autoscaler condition engine determines &lt;strong&gt;when&lt;/strong&gt; an operatorBox: should scale.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;It evaluates a combination of &lt;strong&gt;metric conditions&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;cross‑operator metrics&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;clock windows&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;day‑of‑week rules&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;cron expressions&lt;/strong&gt;, using the same declarative condition DSL used throughout Orkestra.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Conditions are evaluated on every autoscaler tick (&lt;code&gt;interval:&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;br&gt;&#xA;If the declared logic resolves to &lt;strong&gt;true&lt;/strong&gt;, the autoscaler applies the &lt;code&gt;do:&lt;/code&gt; overrides.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;If the logic resolves to &lt;strong&gt;false&lt;/strong&gt; for the entire &lt;code&gt;cooldown:&lt;/code&gt; period, the autoscaler restores the CRD’s baseline.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Autoscaler Metrics</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/autoscaler-metrics/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 28 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/autoscaler-metrics/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The metrics namespace used by the Operator Autoscaler.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The Operator Autoscaler evaluates conditions using a dedicated metrics namespace:&lt;br&gt;&#xA;&lt;code&gt;metrics.*&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;These metrics are collected &lt;strong&gt;inside the Orkestra runtime&lt;/strong&gt;, per CRD, inside each operatorBox.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;They are &lt;strong&gt;in‑memory&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;O(1)&lt;/strong&gt; to read, and require &lt;strong&gt;no API calls&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;no informers&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;no Prometheus&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Metrics are updated continuously by:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;the worker pool&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;the workqueue&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;the reconcile loop&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;the provider subsystem&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;They represent the &lt;strong&gt;true runtime load&lt;/strong&gt; of an operator.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Autoscaler Runtime Behavior</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/autoscaler-runtime-behaviour/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/autoscaler-runtime-behaviour/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;How the autoscaler behaves at runtime.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The autoscaler runs as part of every operatorBox: that declares an &lt;code&gt;autoscale:&lt;/code&gt; block.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;It evaluates conditions, applies overrides, restores baselines, and exposes live metrics — all in‑memory, without API calls or external controllers.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Autoscaling is &lt;strong&gt;safe&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;deterministic&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;fully reversible&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;1-evaluation-loop&#34;&gt;1. Evaluation loop&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The autoscaler runs on a fixed interval:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;interval&lt;/span&gt;: 30s&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;On every tick:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Read local metrics (&lt;code&gt;metrics.*&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Read cross‑operator metrics (&lt;code&gt;cross.&amp;lt;alias&amp;gt;.metrics.*&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Evaluate &lt;code&gt;anyOf&lt;/code&gt; (OR)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Evaluate &lt;code&gt;when&lt;/code&gt; (AND)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Combine results&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-text&#34; data-lang=&#34;text&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;final = anyOf_passes AND when_passes&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;This evaluation is O(1) and entirely in‑memory.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Autoscaler YAML Reference</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/autoscaler-yaml-reference/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/autoscaler-yaml-reference/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The complete schema for &lt;code&gt;operatorBox.autoscale&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The Operator Autoscaler is configured declaratively inside the &lt;code&gt;operatorBox:&lt;/code&gt; block of a CRD’s Katalog entry. This document describes every field, its type, its behavior, and how it interacts with the runtime.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Autoscaling is optional. When omitted, the CRD runs with its declared baseline for its entire lifetime.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;toplevel-structure&#34;&gt;Top‑level structure&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;operatorBox&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;autoscale&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;interval&lt;/span&gt;: &amp;lt;duration&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;cooldown&lt;/span&gt;: &amp;lt;duration&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;conditions&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;anyOf&lt;/span&gt;: [&amp;lt;condition&amp;gt;, ...]&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;:  [&amp;lt;condition&amp;gt;, ...]&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;do&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;workers&lt;/span&gt;: &amp;lt;int&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;queueDepth&lt;/span&gt;: &amp;lt;int&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;resync&lt;/span&gt;: &amp;lt;duration&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;1-interval&#34;&gt;1. &lt;code&gt;interval:&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How often the autoscaler evaluates conditions.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Autoscaler Scenarios</title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/scenarios/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/scenarios/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Real‑world patterns for scaling operatorBox:es.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This document provides practical examples of how to use the Operator Autoscaler to adapt operator performance to real‑world workloads. Each scenario demonstrates a different combination of metric‑based, time‑based, cron‑based, and &lt;strong&gt;cross‑operator‑based&lt;/strong&gt; conditions.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;All examples assume the CRD declares a baseline:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;workers&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#bd93f9&#34;&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;queue&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;maxQueueDepth&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#bd93f9&#34;&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;resync&lt;/span&gt;: 120s&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;The autoscaler always returns to these values when conditions are no longer met.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;1-scale-up-under-load&#34;&gt;1. Scale Up Under Load&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Increase workers and queue depth when the operator is under pressure.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Cross‑Operator Autoscaling  </title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/cross-operator-autoscaling/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 28 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/cross-operator-autoscaling/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;How operators scale based on each other’s load.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Cross‑Operator Autoscaling allows an operatorBox: to scale based on the &lt;strong&gt;runtime metrics of another operator&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;This enables upstream/downstream coordination, pipeline‑wide optimization, and ecosystem‑level behavior — all expressed declaratively in YAML.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This feature is powered by Orkestra’s cross‑operator IPC layer, which exposes:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;.spec&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;.status&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;.labels&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;.metrics&lt;/code&gt; (live runtime metrics)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;…from any referenced operator.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Cross‑operator metrics are read in‑memory (same binary) or via HTTP fallback (cross‑binary / cross‑cluster).&lt;br&gt;&#xA;No API calls, no polling, no external systems.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Cross‑Operator Autoscaling Scenarios  </title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/cross-operator-autoscaling-scenarios/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 25 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/docs/concepts/operator-autoscaler/cross-operator-autoscaling-scenarios/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Real‑world patterns for scaling based on other operators.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Cross‑Operator Autoscaling enables an operatorBox: to scale using &lt;strong&gt;runtime metrics from another operator&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;This unlocks pipeline‑wide coordination, upstream/downstream flow control, and distributed load‑aware behavior.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Below are real‑life scenarios showing how teams use cross‑operator metrics to stabilize pipelines, prevent overload, and optimize throughput.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h1 id=&#34;1-upstream-slows-down-when-downstream-is-overloaded&#34;&gt;1. Upstream Slows Down When Downstream Is Overloaded&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Protect downstream systems from overload.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-yaml&#34; data-lang=&#34;yaml&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;autoscale&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;interval&lt;/span&gt;: 15s&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;cooldown&lt;/span&gt;: 1m&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;conditions&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;field&lt;/span&gt;: cross.db.metrics.queueDepth&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;greaterThan&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;800&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;      - &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;field&lt;/span&gt;: cross.db.metrics.workersBusyPercent&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;greaterThan&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f1fa8c&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;85&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;do&lt;/span&gt;:&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;workers&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#bd93f9&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ff79c6&#34;&gt;queueDepth&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span style=&#34;color:#bd93f9&#34;&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;h3 id=&#34;behavior&#34;&gt;Behavior&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;If the &lt;strong&gt;database operator&lt;/strong&gt; is overwhelmed, the upstream operator slows down&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Prevents cascading failures&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Reduces retries, backpressure, and queue explosions&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3 id=&#34;reallife-use-case&#34;&gt;Real‑life use case&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;API ingestion → DB writer.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;If the DB writer is drowning, ingestion must throttle.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title></title>
      <link>http://localhost:8090/publications/trust-and-failure-model/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:8090/publications/trust-and-failure-model/</guid>
      <description>&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;weight-2&#34;&gt;title: &amp;ldquo;Trust and Failure Model&amp;rdquo;&#xA;date: 2026-05-25&#xA;weight: 2&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Trust in a distributed system is not a feature you add — it is a property that either emerges from the design or does not. Orkestra&amp;rsquo;s approach is to make trustworthy behavior the default at every layer, and to ensure those layers compound rather than exist independently. This is a description of how that works.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-foundational-guarantee&#34;&gt;The foundational guarantee&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Every Orkestra reconciler is level-triggered. It does not replay a log of events — it reads current desired state and drives toward it. This is the same model Kubernetes uses for its own controllers, and it carries the same property: any operation that is interrupted half-way through leaves the cluster in a partial state, not a corrupted one. The next reconcile corrects it.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
